detailed_description: <Video 1> is the persistent LOCATION REFERENCE for the static environment and spatial layout (The tavern is a rectangular room thirty feet east‑west by forty feet north‑south, with a creaking wooden floor of four‑inch planks spaced half an inch apart. The west wall hosts the back door at its center; a wooden latch attaches to a three‑foot wide frame one foot above the floor and opens eastward into the tavern. The south wall contains a stone hearth centered along the wall, twelve feet long with an eight‑foot depth. Its fire surface is six by four feet, surrounded on three sides by a two‑foot high stone surround. East of the hearth’s center lies an oak barrel five feet tall and two feet in diameter against the southern wall. The east wall between the hearth and the north wall runs a crystal shelf eight feet long, rising four feet above floor level and projecting one foot into the room; it holds polished crystal glasses. The north wall hosts a polished counter twelve feet along its length with a three‑foot width projecting into the tavern. Its front edge sits one foot from the northern wall. At the north‑west corner of this counter stands a high bar stool, three feet tall with a seat 1½ by 1 foot in size. West of the back door, two feet east of its center, is a raised back table five by seven feet on a plinth keeping it one foot above floor level. The polished tables—six rectangular pieces each four by six feet—are set across the central area, spaced at least three feet apart to allow passage between them and toward the counter or hearth. Each of the six tables has a lantern stand two feet tall positioned on its surface; the lanterns hold oil lamps that illuminate their immediate areas without blocking movement. The entire space is anchored by the back door and stone hearth, with all other objects fixed in place relative to these anchors). Preserve its architecture, fixed fixtures, entrances, surfaces, persistent furniture, landmarks, and background placement when those areas enter frame. Do not use <Video 1> for characters or current camera framing.  [Shot 1] Live-action cinematic, The scene starts in inside a medieval tavern. Stage this segment as one continuous camera take with no cuts or cutaways; reframe only through continuous camera movement. Preserve each established handheld or movable prop as one distinct physical object; interactions must not duplicate, merge, or substitute containers or other props.

